            <div xml:id="d266">

                <pb facs="0542" n="486" sameAs="frus:frus1876/pg486"/>

                <head>No. 256.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hall
                        to Mr. Cadwalader.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">U.
                                S. Consulate-General</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Havana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1875-12-23"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 23, 1875</date>. (Received
                        December 28.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 304.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The newspapers of this city, of 21st and
                    22d instants, announce <pb facs="0543" n="487" xml:id="pg487"/> the relief of
                    General Count Valmaseda and the appointment of General Jovellar as his
                    successor. That a change was imminent was well understood by all classes, but
                    the appointment of General Jovellar, although hoped for, was hardly expected;
                    naturally, it gives general Satisfaction.</p>
                <p>General Jovellar governed the island from November, 1873, to April, 1874; short
                    as was the term of his administration, he acquired a reputation for integrity,
                    as well as justice and humanity; he appeared to be actuated, also, by a
                    conciliatory spirit toward the Cubans, and if his administration was not a
                    success it was probably due to causes which he could not control.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>HENRY C. HALL.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
